WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.350
Fandoms thrive on creating weird meetings
between different fictional universes.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.870
Aside from Pocky, pizza rolls, and Mountain
Dew: Code Red, throwing together characters

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.830
from different worlds is the fuel that keeps
the fan-fiction world alive.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.119
And if those characters are played by the
same actor, so much the better.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.300
So what would happen if Han Solo and Indiana
Jones happened to inhabit the same reality?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.490
Now that Disney owns both properties, anything
is possible, but what if we told you it already

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:27.490
happened?

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.430
A long time ago...

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.640
Star Wars takes place "a long time ago," but
what does that really mean for us Earth viewers?

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.059
Star Wars fiction always throws around terms
like BBY or "Before the Battle of Yavin."

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:42.850
But none of that means anything to us humans
and our local time.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:47.710
Some timelines, citing Expanded Universe comic
tie-ins, place the events of A New Hope around

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:52.870
the Earth-year 1800, and an important part
of that theory comes from one bonkers Han

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:57.410
Solo-Indiana Jones crossover in 2004's Star
Wars Tales #19.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:03.830
The short story "Into the Great Unknown",
written by W. Haden Blackman, begins, well,

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.140
a long time ago.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.820
It starts off fairly normal, but things get
pretty weird, pretty fast.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.430
In a galaxy...

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.650
The story starts with Han and Chewy on board
the Millennium Falcon, escaping some Imperial

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.000
Star Destroyers, as usual.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.850
Because their ship is a piece of junk, it's
not working right, and they make the jump

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.490
to hyperspace blindly.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.290
Han remarks that the ship's computers have
been on the fritz since they left a moon called

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.900
Hovan 99, a place rarely mentioned in any
Star Wars canon.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.259
Already, it appears we're getting references
to weird Earth things, because football player

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:42.009
Chris Hovan was player #99 on the Minnesota
Vikings around the time this comic was written.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:43.009
Coincidence?

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:44.700
Eh, probably.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.630
Not so far away...

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.841
The pair end up in an unfamiliar system with
lots of dead planets, and one blue one, so

00:01:51.841 --> 00:01:54.000
you can already see where this is going.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.700
The Falcon takes out a whole ton of trees
in a catastrophic crash landing, attracting

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.100
the attention of some local Native Americans.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.770
Han remarks that their landing spot looks
a lot like Endor, so it's likely that the

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.509
ship landed in the redwood forests of California,
where Endor was actually filmed.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:15.840
Star Wars has officially, in an Elseworlds
sort of way, reached Earth.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.459
Under attack

00:02:17.459 --> 00:02:21.379
There's really no indication about whether
or not Han and Chewy hopped through time in

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:24.989
their journey, but they're now deep within
dangerous territory.

00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:29.329
Han is immediately attacked with spears and
arrows, as we see Chewy go on the kind of

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:34.090
head-crushing, rage-filled rampage that we
all wish we saw in The Force Awakens.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:39.169
Han gets an arrow to the shoulder, gut, leg,
and one apparently pierces his chest as well.

00:02:39.169 --> 00:02:44.510
For a goofy, lighthearted comic book story,
it's pretty horrific and brutal.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.400
Chewsquatcha

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.830
Chewy's cries of agony at Han's unfortunate
end are taken to be the calls of a sasquatch

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.910
by the nearby human attackers.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:57.180
This means that there are already legends,
if not sightings, of some creatures pretty

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.329
similar to Chewy already on Earth.

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:04.349
This raises the question of Wookiees and sasquatches
being products of convergent evolution, or

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:08.620
if our familiar 'squatches are just Wookiees
that were dropped off here on our dirty Outer

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.891
Rim planet a long time ago.

00:03:09.891 --> 00:03:12.999
A little while ago...

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:17.919
The story then jumps 126 years forward, and
we see a Native American guide leading an

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:22.549
unseen figure towards the home of some unknown
creature, tracking footprints.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:28.499
It's quickly revealed that this figure is
a circa-1935 Doctor Henry "Indiana" Jones.

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:32.739
Along with Temple of Doom's Short Round, they
discover the Millennium Falcon, as well as

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:35.559
Han Solo's skeletonized corpse.

00:03:35.559 --> 00:03:40.189
Even though Jones is essentially a professional
artifact thief, he gets a weird feeling about

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:45.269
the whole thing and just decides to abandon
his whole adventure right there, spaceship,

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:46.479
sasquatch, and all.

00:03:46.479 --> 00:03:47.949
"You could've kept it."

00:03:47.949 --> 00:03:51.359
"Ah, what for, they'd just put in a museum..."

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:53.150
The great unknown

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.510
The story ends with Indy giving up on his
hunt for Bigfoot, getting creeped out about

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.639
the whole thing.

00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:03.239
The Earth portrayed in the comic is apparently
not a curious place — no one else ever notices

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.870
the Millennium Falcon, and Chewbacca continues
to wander the California forests.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.839
According to the old Expanded Universe, Chewy
was roughly 200 years old during the events

00:04:12.839 --> 00:04:16.139
of the Original Trilogy, a fact that the new
movies confirm.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:18.289
"You're 190 years old?"

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:19.910
"You look great!"

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:26.539
At the age of 326 and outside of his native
habitat, comic book Chewbacca is nearing the

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:28.419
400-year life expectancy of Wookiees.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:32.970
The awful implication is that he's going to
live the rest of his life alone on Earth,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.840
which is a pretty dark story by Star Wars
standards.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.830
But at least they made him into a pretty cool
myth.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.370
Not the first time

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.199
This comic isn't the only time that Star Wars
lore crossed over with Indiana Jones.

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:50.560
If you watch Raiders of the Lost Ark carefully,
you'll spot a hieroglyph of R2-D2 and C-3PO

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.990
carved into a pillar, a reference that's so
well-loved it even made its way into an Indiana

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.800
Jones LEGO playset.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.940
But that's not all.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.580
Temple of Doom has a nightclub evidently founded
by Jedi Master Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.340
And you can even find the Ark of the Covenant
in an episode of the animated Clone Wars..

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.129
Now, we're just wondering where Blade Runner
fits in.

00:05:10.129 --> 00:05:11.270
Thanks for watching!

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.800
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.870
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

